---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import InterestForm from "../components/InterestForm.astro";
---

<Base
  title="Become a resident"
  description="Andorra residency: official letters A.1, J.1, D.1, B.1, D.3, D.4 and the 2026 investment figures."
  path="/become-a-resident"
>
  <section class="page-header">
    <h1>Become a resident</h1>
    <p>
      People searching “passive residency” or “residencia pasiva” usually
      mean D.1, residence without work. Some sites call the investment
      route a golden visa; that is not an official Andorran name.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="prose">
    <div class="notice">
      <p>
        Use the live procedure on
        <a
          href="https://www.govern.ad/ca/tematiques/immigracio/residencia-i-treball"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener">Govern.ad</a
        >
        and the 2026 text of
        <a
          href="https://bopadocuments.blob.core.windows.net/bopa-documents/038015/pdf/CGL_2026_02_12_10_11_12.pdf"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener">Law 2/2026 in the BOPA</a
        >
        before you act. The letters below are the Government’s.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>The official letters</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Letter</th>
          <th>What it is</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>A.1</td>
          <td>Residence and work for an Andorran employer.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>J.1</td>
          <td>Residence and work on your own account.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>D.1</td>
          <td>Residence without taking local employment.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>B.1</td>
          <td>Family reunification.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>C.1</td>
          <td>Frontier work.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>D.3</td>
          <td>Digital nomad. Still on Govern.ad.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>D.4</td>
          <td>Entrepreneur programmes. Still on Govern.ad.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Jane Whittaker map said once: her A is official D.1 without work;
      her B is D.1.2; her C is D.1.3; her D is official J.1. Those
      private letters are a map, said once. They are not the law. This
      site uses official codes. Her guide is
      <a
        href="https://www.livinginandorra.com/residency_options.html"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener">livinginandorra.com</a
      >.
    </p>

    <h2>Residence and work</h2>
    <p>
      A.1 usually needs a job contract with a real Andorran company, and
      a place in this year's quota. J.1 is a separate first
      authorisation if you will work on your own account. Both have
      document lists on the official immigration pages. More on
      <a href="/self-employed-residence">J.1</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The first A.1 or J.1 card is typically issued for one year. Later
      renewals run longer. Nationals of states with a convention may see
      a different cycle.
    </p>
    <p>
      Govern.ad still publishes the
      <a
        href="https://www.govern.ad/ca/tematiques/immigracio/residencia-i-treball/residencia-i-treball-per-compte-propi-autoritzacio-inicial"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener">J.1 page</a
      >
      and a
      <a
        href="https://www.govern.ad/ca/tematiques/immigracio/residencia-i-treball/reserva-autoritzacio-de-residencia-i-treball-per-compte-propi"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener">J reservation</a
      >
      step. A
      <a
        href="https://www.govern.ad/ca/w/aprovada-una-nova-quota-d-autoritzacions-d-immigracio-de-residencia-i-treball-per-compte-propi-i-de-treball-sense-residencia"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener">Govern news note</a
      >
      describes Decret 75/2026 setting a general self-employed quota of
      200 authorisations: 150 for foreign-investment cases, 50 for liberal
      professions. Check whether a place is actually being issued before
      you plan around anyone’s rumour that the route is “closed”.
    </p>

    <h2>Residence without work</h2>
    <p>
      D.1 covers people who will live in Andorra without taking local
      employment. The Government’s page currently names D.1.2, D.1.3 and
      D.1.4. Digital-nomad (D.3) and entrepreneur-programme (D.4)
      authorisations sit on the same official residence index. Longer
      notes:
      <a href="/residence-without-work">D.1</a>,
      <a href="/digital-nomad">D.3</a>,
      <a href="/entrepreneur-residence">D.4</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Several of those pages still say principal and effective residence
      for at least 90 days per calendar year. Tax residence under article
      8 of Law 5/2014 is a different test: more than 183 days, or the
      centre of economic interests. That is on
      <a href="/tax-residence">tax residence</a>.
      The official texts state the conditions. They do not describe how
      days are checked.
    </p>

    <h3>What Law 2/2026 says for non-lucrative residence</h3>
    <p>
      Law 2/2026 of 22 January was published in the BOPA on 12 February
      2026 and entered into force on 13 February 2026. Article 96, as
      printed there, requires the principal holder of a residence without
      lucrative activity to invest, permanently and effectively, at least
      <strong>1,000,000 euros</strong> in named Andorran assets: property;
      equity in resident companies; certain debt or collective-investment
      instruments of resident issuers, for a maximum of 36 months before
      the sum must move to another permitted class; public-administration
      debt; life-insurance products with resident insurers; or
      unremunerated AFA deposits.
    </p>
    <p>
      If that investment is made wholly or partly in property, the same
      article requires more than <strong>800,000 euros</strong> on each
      immovable unit. The million-euro total still applies. A purchase
      above 800,000 euros but below 1,000,000 euros leaves a balance that
      must sit in other permitted assets.
    </p>
    <p>
      The same article reduces the investment to
      <strong>400,000 euros</strong> where it is made, directly or
      indirectly, permanently and effectively, in the Housing Fund (Fons
      d’Habitatge), “in accordance with the applicable regulations”. Think
      of that last figure as a legal possibility, not a counter you can
      walk up to.
    </p>
    <p>
      Article 96 also requires an income to the Andorran Financial
      Authority of <strong>50,000 euros</strong> for the principal and
      <strong>12,000 euros</strong> for each dependant who takes the same
      status. The BOPA text says those sums are definitive and not
      refundable, unless the initial immigration authorisation is refused.
      Read the article that matches your subtype with counsel.
    </p>

    <h3>Card-issue tariffs on Govern.ad</h3>
    <p>
      The Government’s
      <a
        href="https://www.govern.ad/ca/tematiques/immigracio/altres-tramits/tarifes"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener">immigration tariffs page</a
      >
      currently lists 3,000 euros for delivery of a residence-without-work
      authorisation, 1,000 euros for a dependant resident, and 500 euros
      for renewal of residence without work. Other permit types on the
      same table carry different amounts. Confirm the line that matches
      your file.
    </p>

    <h2>Family, study and other statuses</h2>
    <p>
      Family reunification (B.1), study and research (F), temporary
      seasonal authorisations (G.1) and frontier work (C.1) are published
      on the same Government section. B.1 is
      <a href="/family-reunification">family reunification</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>A house is a separate tax</h2>
    <p>
      A house is not a permit. Non-resident purchases need
      foreign-investment authorisation. A Government news note of 26
      February 2026, giving effect to Law 2/2026, states that the
      foreign-investment property tax moves from 3% to 6% on a first
      property, and from 5% to 10% on a second home. Residents who cannot
      show three or more years of effective and permanent residence in
      the ten years before the application stay in that tax net (Decret
      58/2026). Detail:
      <a href="/buying-property">buying as a non-resident</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>What the file usually contains</h2>
    <p>
      Applications go to the Servei d’Immigració. Official pages describe
      identity documents, legalised or apostilled criminal-record
      certificates, proof of accommodation, civil-status documents, and
      for workers a contract. A medical review is part of the published
      file. After a grant, you register with the Comú of the parish of
      residence. The current A.1 page gives three months for that.
    </p>

